1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,879
29 October.

2
00:00:02,120 --> 00:00:08,561
A Rafale fighter, here for trials, comes down on India's newest aircraft carrier, INS Vikrant.

3
00:00:08,788 --> 00:00:13,838
This deck is missing something that India's first carrier had, back in 1961.

4
00:00:14,146 --> 00:00:15,069
A catapult.

5
00:00:15,281 --> 00:00:18,241
The Rafale was built to be thrown off a ship by one.

6
00:00:18,455 --> 00:00:20,360
Vikrant has a ramp instead.

7
00:00:20,484 --> 00:00:22,275
The jet can leave by that ramp.

8
00:00:22,419 --> 00:00:24,053
It passed that test in Goa.

9
00:00:24,249 --> 00:00:25,365
But think about this.

10
00:00:25,477 --> 00:00:29,198
65 years ago, India was throwing jets off a ship.

11
00:00:29,377 --> 00:00:35,854
Today the Navy wants a catapult again — and the one India has shown so far, sits on the back of a lorry.

12
00:00:35,974 --> 00:00:37,481
So what happened in between?

13
00:00:37,629 --> 00:00:39,854
And here is something you would not guess.

14
00:00:39,933 --> 00:00:44,630
The electricity to throw a fighter jet costs you less than ₹200.

15
00:00:44,721 --> 00:00:47,652
Yet it is one of the hardest machines on earth.

16
00:00:47,764 --> 00:00:50,165
By the end of this film, you will see why.

17
00:00:50,406 --> 00:00:52,586
Come and stand on Vikrant's deck with me.

18
00:00:52,747 --> 00:00:57,524
In front of you, the deck curves upward, like a flyover that ends in the sky.

19
00:00:57,672 --> 00:00:59,880
Do you see any moving parts on that ramp?

20
00:01:00,099 --> 00:01:00,643
None.

21
00:01:00,775 --> 00:01:05,113
The jet opens its engines fully, runs, and the curve throws it up.

22
00:01:05,235 --> 00:01:06,467
Nothing you can break.

23
00:01:06,502 --> 00:01:07,854
Nothing you must repair.

24
00:01:08,006 --> 00:01:10,610
You have to admit, it is a clever idea.

25
00:01:10,737 --> 00:01:11,824
But here's the catch.

26
00:01:11,916 --> 00:01:16,094
On a ramp, the jet must lift itself, with its own engines.

27
00:01:16,150 --> 00:01:17,821
So you take off lighter.

28
00:01:17,949 --> 00:01:21,003
You carry less fuel, or fewer weapons, or both.

29
00:01:21,132 --> 00:01:22,097
How much lighter?

30
00:01:22,230 --> 00:01:24,319
The Navy has not published that number.

31
00:01:24,357 --> 00:01:26,180
So I won't invent one for you.

32
00:01:26,349 --> 00:01:28,600
What nobody argues about is this.

33
00:01:28,718 --> 00:01:32,719
You cannot get a heavy, slow aircraft off a ramp at all.

34
00:01:32,829 --> 00:01:35,344
And which heavy aircraft matters most at sea?

35
00:01:35,443 --> 00:01:36,663
The flying radar.

36
00:01:36,787 --> 00:01:41,414
A plane with a big dish on its back, that sees trouble long before your ship can.

37
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:44,124
India's carriers do that job with a helicopter.

38
00:01:44,259 --> 00:01:44,997
It works.

39
00:01:45,066 --> 00:01:50,488
But ask naval experts, and they will tell you, a helicopter cannot do it as well as a plane.

40
00:01:50,627 --> 00:01:52,509
So the ramp has a price.

41
00:01:52,648 --> 00:01:54,261
Then why did India choose it?

42
00:01:54,409 --> 00:01:59,595
To answer that, you must first see the catapult we already had, and how we let it go.

43
00:01:59,869 --> 00:02:02,943
Belfast, 4 March 1961.

44
00:02:03,078 --> 00:02:08,543
India commissions its first aircraft carrier, bought from Britain and finished in that shipyard.

45
00:02:08,636 --> 00:02:10,521
Her name, too, is Vikrant.

46
00:02:10,665 --> 00:02:13,164
And under her deck is a steam catapult.

47
00:02:13,335 --> 00:02:15,080
How does steam throw a jet?

48
00:02:15,232 --> 00:02:16,851
You already own a small one.

49
00:02:16,901 --> 00:02:18,552
The pressure cooker in your kitchen.

50
00:02:18,650 --> 00:02:23,129
Steam builds up behind the weight, and when it goes, it goes with force.

51
00:02:23,196 --> 00:02:29,187
On the ship, that steam drives a piston along a tube under the deck, and the piston drags the jet.

52
00:02:29,286 --> 00:02:31,148
For nine years, it works.

53
00:02:31,226 --> 00:02:36,449
Then, in June 1970, in Bombay, the engineers find cracks in a boiler.

54
00:02:36,584 --> 00:02:37,647
Not one crack.

55
00:02:37,647 --> 00:02:40,234
Many, in the drums that hold the water.

56
00:02:40,329 --> 00:02:41,691
You cannot weld them.

57
00:02:41,878 --> 00:02:44,599
And a lasting repair is not possible in India.

58
00:02:44,863 --> 00:02:46,066
Do you see what that means?

59
00:02:46,231 --> 00:02:50,445
The same steam that turns the propellers, also fires the catapult.

60
00:02:50,537 --> 00:02:56,394
If you push those boilers too hard, in the words of Admiral Arun Prakash, they could simply blow up.

61
00:02:56,635 --> 00:02:58,260
Would you send that ship to war?

62
00:02:58,469 --> 00:03:00,803
So the ship is held to 14 knots.

63
00:03:00,867 --> 00:03:03,157
That is about 26 km/h.

64
00:03:03,389 --> 00:03:04,713
Too slow for a carrier.

65
00:03:04,816 --> 00:03:08,515
And some very senior officers say, leave her out of the next war.

66
00:03:08,759 --> 00:03:11,860
The Navy chief, Admiral Nanda, will not have it.

67
00:03:11,960 --> 00:03:18,388
If Vikrant sits out a war, he says, she will be called a white elephant, and naval flying will be written off.

68
00:03:18,525 --> 00:03:21,739
So it comes down to the engine room, and to one man.

69
00:03:21,857 --> 00:03:24,970
Commander Benoy Roy Chowdhury, the ship's engineer.

70
00:03:25,056 --> 00:03:26,851
His shipmates call him Bilu.

71
00:03:27,006 --> 00:03:30,063
What would you do with a cracked boiler, and no spare?

72
00:03:30,211 --> 00:03:32,878
Bilu and his team put steel bands around it.

73
00:03:32,984 --> 00:03:36,184
They reset the safety valves, to accept more risk.

74
00:03:36,278 --> 00:03:38,836
And they keep the boiler room almost empty.

75
00:03:39,011 --> 00:03:43,072
Because if a drum bursts, that room fills with superheated steam.

76
00:03:43,358 --> 00:03:44,228
It holds.

77
00:03:44,350 --> 00:03:51,179
On 4 December 1971, Sea Hawk jets leave Vikrant's catapult, and strike Cox's Bazar.

78
00:03:51,303 --> 00:03:56,729
And for his work in that boiler room, Bilu is given the Vir Chakra, a medal for bravery.

79
00:03:56,814 --> 00:03:59,313
So India did not only have a catapult.

80
00:03:59,466 --> 00:04:03,180
India kept one alive with steel bands, and took it to war.

81
00:04:03,379 --> 00:04:04,908
Then how did we lose it?

82
00:04:05,059 --> 00:04:10,219
In the 1980s, the Navy bought the Sea Harrier, a jet that needs no catapult.

83
00:04:10,328 --> 00:04:16,444
The last planes that still needed the catapult, the old propeller planes, retired in 1989.

84
00:04:16,592 --> 00:04:19,443
So the catapult came out, and a ramp went on.

85
00:04:19,592 --> 00:04:20,557
You can see why.

86
00:04:20,662 --> 00:04:21,889
It made sense then.

87
00:04:22,019 --> 00:04:27,545
And when India designed its own carrier, the new Vikrant, she was drawn around a ramp jet too.

88
00:04:27,702 --> 00:04:29,646
Her engines are gas turbines.

89
00:04:29,690 --> 00:04:31,667
They make no steam for a catapult.

90
00:04:31,783 --> 00:04:35,870
And naval analysts say there is no room under that ramp to add one now.

91
00:04:36,097 --> 00:04:37,647
So steam is gone.

92
00:04:37,794 --> 00:04:41,634
If you want to throw a jet again, you have to do it with electricity.

93
00:04:41,824 --> 00:04:43,285
Look up at your ceiling fan.

94
00:04:43,381 --> 00:04:44,546
Do you know what turns it?

95
00:04:44,672 --> 00:04:45,993
An induction motor.

96
00:04:46,137 --> 00:04:50,885
Coils of wire in a circle, and a magnetic pull that chases round and round.

97
00:04:50,989 --> 00:04:54,235
Now cut that circle in your mind, and unroll it flat.

98
00:04:54,358 --> 00:04:59,257
You get a straight line of coils, about 100 metres long, laid under the deck.

99
00:04:59,388 --> 00:05:01,348
The pull no longer goes round.

100
00:05:01,444 --> 00:05:04,584
It runs straight ahead, and it takes the jet with it.

101
00:05:04,693 --> 00:05:07,289
Only the coils beside the jet are switched on.

102
00:05:07,388 --> 00:05:09,890
A wave of force, racing down the deck.

103
00:05:10,001 --> 00:05:10,974
Now count with me.

104
00:05:11,172 --> 00:05:11,722
One.

105
00:05:12,121 --> 00:05:12,565
Two.

106
00:05:13,109 --> 00:05:13,720
Three.

107
00:05:13,912 --> 00:05:21,595
By the time you finish counting, a 30-tonne fighter has gone from standing still, to 240 km/h.

108
00:05:21,884 --> 00:05:24,088
And what does that take, in electricity?

109
00:05:24,322 --> 00:05:26,025
Here is the number I promised you.

110
00:05:26,213 --> 00:05:30,474
The energy in that moving jet is about 19 units of electricity.

111
00:05:30,638 --> 00:05:34,373
An average Indian home uses that much in about six days.

112
00:05:34,518 --> 00:05:37,418
On your bill, it is well under ₹200.

113
00:05:37,568 --> 00:05:39,202
Then where is the difficulty?

114
00:05:39,416 --> 00:05:40,993
In the three seconds.

115
00:05:41,098 --> 00:05:44,136
Your home sips that energy over six days.

116
00:05:44,153 --> 00:05:47,322
The catapult must pour it out in three seconds.

117
00:05:47,379 --> 00:05:50,636
At its peak, that is close to 50 megawatts.

118
00:05:50,642 --> 00:05:54,342
More than half of what all four of Vikrant's engines make together.

119
00:05:54,485 --> 00:05:57,306
Can you take that from a ship's engines, in an instant?

120
00:05:57,475 --> 00:05:57,945
No.

121
00:05:58,096 --> 00:05:59,465
So you save up.

122
00:05:59,523 --> 00:06:06,895
Between launches, you spin up great steel machines, each one over 36 tonnes, like giant spinning tops.

123
00:06:06,964 --> 00:06:11,678
Then you pull the energy back out of that spin, and that becomes the push.

124
00:06:11,760 --> 00:06:17,415
And you must do this again every 45 seconds, for years, on a ship at sea.

125
00:06:17,558 --> 00:06:19,562
So now you know the real machine.

126
00:06:19,680 --> 00:06:20,511
Not a motor.

127
00:06:20,612 --> 00:06:24,319
A power station, that works for three seconds at a time.

128
00:06:25,511 --> 00:06:27,106
Has anyone made it work?

129
00:06:27,315 --> 00:06:28,381
Two navies have.

130
00:06:28,482 --> 00:06:31,276
And their stories run in opposite directions.

131
00:06:31,472 --> 00:06:32,753
Let's start with China.

132
00:06:32,989 --> 00:06:35,393
China never sent a steam catapult to sea.

133
00:06:35,593 --> 00:06:40,441
From 2016, it was throwing a test jet from catapults built on land.

134
00:06:40,584 --> 00:06:46,987
Nine years later, in November 2025, it commissioned the carrier Fujian, with electric catapults.

135
00:06:47,115 --> 00:06:50,653
Fujian has already thrown a flying radar plane into the air.

136
00:06:50,800 --> 00:06:52,607
And she is not nuclear powered.

137
00:06:52,789 --> 00:06:55,429
So you do not need a reactor for this machine.

138
00:06:55,587 --> 00:06:57,113
You need stored power.

139
00:06:57,364 --> 00:06:59,495
And America, which invented it?

140
00:06:59,645 --> 00:07:03,129
Its first ship with electric catapults is the USS Ford.

141
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:08,003
The American Navy wanted more than 4,000 launches between serious failures.

142
00:07:08,099 --> 00:07:11,839
In 2020, the testers measured 181.

143
00:07:12,023 --> 00:07:14,898
In 2022, 614.

144
00:07:14,989 --> 00:07:15,966
Far short.

145
00:07:16,120 --> 00:07:18,249
But the ship did not stop flying.

146
00:07:18,376 --> 00:07:23,440
By the programme's own count, the Ford has made more than 36,000 launches.

147
00:07:23,533 --> 00:07:26,139
Steam catapults had many decades to mature.

148
00:07:26,323 --> 00:07:27,917
This one is still young.

149
00:07:28,040 --> 00:07:34,087
Even so, this August, the American President ordered a plan to go back to steam on a future carrier.

150
00:07:34,192 --> 00:07:38,524
The company that builds the electric one has asked the government to think again.

151
00:07:38,636 --> 00:07:41,757
As this film is made, nothing final has been announced.

152
00:07:41,847 --> 00:07:46,254
So the inventor is in two minds, and the newcomer is already flying.

153
00:07:46,494 --> 00:07:47,889
Where does that leave India?

154
00:07:48,049 --> 00:07:54,603
In Pune, in Maharashtra, engineers at a DRDO laboratory are building India's own electric catapult.

155
00:07:54,764 --> 00:07:55,762
Remember the lorry?

156
00:07:55,924 --> 00:07:56,635
This is it.

157
00:07:56,715 --> 00:08:00,093
At the Bengaluru air show last year, it stood on a trailer.

158
00:08:00,235 --> 00:08:05,751
As reported, it is meant to throw up to 400 kilos, over about 17 metres.

159
00:08:05,876 --> 00:08:11,149
I say as reported, because DRDO itself has published very little about it.

160
00:08:11,219 --> 00:08:14,315
Now put the Navy's need beside it, and you see the gap.

161
00:08:14,406 --> 00:08:15,839
Not 400 kilos.

162
00:08:15,949 --> 00:08:17,050
30 tonnes.

163
00:08:17,136 --> 00:08:18,625
Not 17 metres.

164
00:08:18,676 --> 00:08:19,957
About 100.

165
00:08:20,012 --> 00:08:22,412
That is 75 times the weight.

166
00:08:22,565 --> 00:08:27,918
A full-size one, say officials quoted in the defence press, is a decade or more away.

167
00:08:28,155 --> 00:08:32,062
You might ask me, Kishore, who is stopping India from building its own?

168
00:08:32,418 --> 00:08:33,147
Nobody.

169
00:08:33,234 --> 00:08:34,690
Nobody is stopping us.

170
00:08:34,849 --> 00:08:38,374
But look at what China had, that India does not have yet.

171
00:08:38,517 --> 00:08:40,952
A full-size test track on land.

172
00:08:41,079 --> 00:08:43,133
And a ship waiting for the machine.

173
00:08:43,313 --> 00:08:47,418
The Navy's own 15-year plan asks for two electric catapults.

174
00:08:47,512 --> 00:08:54,021
But the next carrier close to approval, at an estimated ₹40,000 crore, is a repeat of Vikrant.

175
00:08:54,170 --> 00:08:55,024
With a ramp.

176
00:08:55,200 --> 00:08:56,828
Then why not just buy one?

177
00:08:56,985 --> 00:09:01,857
You may remember the reports in 2017, that America was ready to sell it to India.

178
00:09:02,083 --> 00:09:03,119
Nothing was signed.

179
00:09:03,219 --> 00:09:06,529
Later, America cleared the same system for France.

180
00:09:06,606 --> 00:09:13,083
The estimate for one ship's set, with the wires that stop a landing jet, was $1.3 billion.

181
00:09:13,157 --> 00:09:16,804
Around ₹11,000 crore, before you build the ship.

182
00:09:16,932 --> 00:09:18,553
So let me put it to you plainly.

183
00:09:18,710 --> 00:09:20,156
The jets are ordered.

184
00:09:20,308 --> 00:09:24,229
26 Rafales, for about ₹63,000 crore.

185
00:09:24,344 --> 00:09:26,627
The catapult is the size of a lorry.

186
00:09:26,723 --> 00:09:28,742
And the ship is not approved.

187
00:09:28,864 --> 00:09:32,694
Until that changes, India's Rafales will fly from ramps.

188
00:09:32,841 --> 00:09:34,785
Now look at what India does have.

189
00:09:34,921 --> 00:09:38,780
This is the Navy that took a catapult to war on a cracked boiler.

190
00:09:38,892 --> 00:09:42,540
And India designed and built the new Vikrant itself.

191
00:09:42,643 --> 00:09:47,348
What is missing is a decision, a test track, and ten patient years.

192
00:09:47,483 --> 00:09:49,252
And you should hear the other side too.

193
00:09:49,314 --> 00:09:54,643
Some of India's own defence leaders have said, submarines and the Army must come first.

194
00:09:54,701 --> 00:09:57,962
That argument is one reason the ship still waits.

195
00:09:58,086 --> 00:09:59,633
So what should you carry home?

196
00:09:59,769 --> 00:10:02,460
The motor is a cousin of your ceiling fan.

197
00:10:02,545 --> 00:10:05,148
The energy is six days of your home.

198
00:10:05,273 --> 00:10:07,772
Everything hard is in the three seconds.

199
00:10:07,886 --> 00:10:09,091
You can buy the jet.

200
00:10:09,230 --> 00:10:10,688
You have to build the throw.

201
00:10:10,913 --> 00:10:12,441
Three things for you to watch.

202
00:10:12,514 --> 00:10:14,319
The decision on the next carrier.

203
00:10:14,405 --> 00:10:17,527
The first Indian Rafale, due around 2029.

204
00:10:17,585 --> 00:10:20,916
And the day that lorry in Pune throws something heavier.

205
00:10:21,095 --> 00:10:24,440
If this made a hard subject simple for you, subscribe.

206
00:10:24,551 --> 00:10:28,621
When that decision comes, you and I will open up that deck together.

207
00:10:28,760 --> 00:10:30,068
And tell me in the comments.

208
00:10:30,107 --> 00:10:35,676
If that ₹40,000 crore were your money, what would you build — a ramp, or a catapult?

209
00:10:35,840 --> 00:10:36,574
Next time.

210
00:10:36,706 --> 00:10:39,885
A submarine that cannot hear what is right behind it.

211
00:10:40,021 --> 00:10:41,807
This is Be Practical with Kishore.

212
00:10:41,950 --> 00:10:42,960
Stay practical.
